description:
Do what `GetProcAddress` does, on a DLL image already loaded in memory (so every RVA is simply an index into `image`):

```cpp
struct Export { std::uint32_t rva; std::string forwarder; };

std::string dllName(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& image, std::uint32_t dirRva);
std::optional<Export> findByName(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& image, std::uint32_t dirRva, std::uint32_t dirSize, std::string_view name);
std::optional<Export> findByOrdinal(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& image, std::uint32_t dirRva, std::uint32_t dirSize, std::uint32_t ordinal);
```

The export directory at `dirRva` is laid out as in lesson 1.04 (`Name` at +12, `Base` at +16, `NumberOfFunctions` at +20, `NumberOfNames` at +24, then the three array RVAs at +28, +32 and +36).

- **dllName** reads the zero-terminated name.
- **findByName** finds the name in the sorted names array with a **binary search** (compare as `std::strcmp` would), takes the 2-byte index at the same position of the name-ordinals array, and returns the function RVA at that index.
- **findByOrdinal** uses `ordinal - Base` as the index into the functions array.
- In both, a missing name, an index outside the functions array or a function RVA of 0 gives `std::nullopt`. If the function RVA lies inside the export directory (`dirRva <= rva < dirRva + dirSize`), it is a **forwarder**: set `forwarder` to the string at that RVA.

